===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|picalilli}}

===Etymology===
{{unk|en}}, 1845, presumably variant of {{m|en|pickle}},<ref name="mw">{{R:Merriam Webster Online}}</ref> which itself is from {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|pekille||spicy sauce served with meat or fish}} or {{m|enm|pikel}}, from {{etyl|dum|en}} {{m|dum|pekel||brine}}.

Alternatively, may be related to {{m|en|paco lilla||ginger-spiced pickle}}, in 18th century British cookbook ''{{w|The Art of Cookery}}'' (1747–1843; e.g., 1805 edition) by {{w|Hannah Glasse}} (1708–1770), also referred to as “Indian pickles”,<ref>''{{w|The Art of Cookery}},'' {{w|Hannah Glasse}}, 1998 facsimile edition of 1805 edition, [http://books.google.com/books?id=BJY58UqSEMUC&pg=PA284&lpg=PA284&dq=%22paco+lilla%22 p. 284]</ref> which suggests an {{etyl|inc|en}} source; compare {{m|en|mulligatawny}}, of Tamil origin, which also features turmeric prominently, and {{m|en|curry}}, of Tamil or Middle English origin (or blend). No relation to {{m|en|Piccadilly}}.<ref name="mw" />

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|ɪli|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|piccalillis}}

# {{lb|en|British}} A [[yellow]] [[pickle]] [[relish]] made from [[cauliflower]], vegetable [[marrow]], and other vegetables, pickled with [[vinegar]], [[salt]], [[sugar]], and spiced with [[mustard]], [[turmeric]], and other spices.
# {{lb|en|US}} A [[pickle]], typically on a base of chopped green (unripe) tomatoes, but sometimes finely-chopped [[gherkin]]s, and possibly including other vegetables.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|American pickle relish}} {{l|en|chow-chow}}, {{l|en|India relish}}
* {{sense|American green finely chopped gherkin-based pickle}} {{l|en|relish}} {{qualifier|particularly if used on [[Chicago-style hot dogs]]}}

